I Introduction – From the Science to the Politics of Climate Change
This paper will provide critical perspectives on carbon pricing in the UK. Carbon dioxide (CO2) emissions pricing or the shorter more convenient form ‘carbon pricing’ (CP) that is frequently used as in this paper, refers to mechanisms such as carbon taxes, emissions trading systems (ETSs) or ‘cap-and-trade systems’ or various hybrid systems devised for tackling climate change (Kirkpatrick, 2022). The CP systems that are operating in a number of countries in the world have been introduced after many years of scientific developments to produce and analyse evidence and significant international political initiatives. Awareness and discussion of CO2 as one particular ‘greenhouse gas’, has increased significantly in the Twenty First Century because of concerns about the effects of human activity-related CO2 emissions on climate change. It took a long time for science to influence government policy and then for that policy to be reflected in CP mechanisms.
Scientific studies on what became known as the ‘greenhouse effect’ can be traced back to the nineteenth century, for example in papers by Joseph Fourier who proposed the
hypothesis that gases in the earth’s atmosphere must create heat-trapping barriers (Fourier, 1824 and Fourier, 1837) and Eunice Newton Foote who performed experimental work in 1856 (Foote 1856 and Shapiro, 2021) then John Tyndall produced precision equipment for experiments between 1859 and 1861 with findings that showed the powerful heat trapping effect of CO2 (Tyndall, 1861), and then there were later contributions such as those by Svante Arrhenius in 1896 who created perhaps the first model of climate change and
predicted that a doubling of the amount of CO2 in the Earth’s atmosphere would increase the Earth’s temperature by between 5 and 6 degrees Centigrade (Arrhenius et al., 2008). The estimations of the rate of change of global warming led to significant upward revisions of the greenhouse effect as more data was produced and measurement improved especially in the latter part of the Twentieth Century.
If the Nineteenth Century research provided the theory and models for analysis of greenhouse gases and heat-trapping effects on climate change, then the Twentieth Century was significant for the considerable improvements in data collection and measurement of the levels of CO2 emissions as well as more accurate data on changes in global temperatures. The 1960s and 1970s witnessed the development of satellite technology such as NASA’s Nimbus satellite that provided global atmospheric temperature
measurements (NASA, 2025). In the 1970s with the increasing media attention responding to increased availability of data on a variety of environmental issues - particularly pollution although climate change was also on the menu - there was increased activism with rallies and demonstrations in some cases, for example the first Earth Day took place in 1970 (Earth Day, 2025).
The 1980s and 1990s saw the rapid advancement of computer technology with advanced software helping to produce more powerful models for analysis and predictions of climate change effects. In the first quarter of the Twenty First Century the scientific evidence has continued to grow with more critical analysis of aspects such as regional variations. The development of the international politics of climate change can also be traced back to the 1970s with significant involvement by organisations such as the United Nations and the


