II International Institutional Climate Change Policy Drivers
The United Nations provided the forum for different nations to discuss the global environment in June 1972 when it held the UN Conference on the Human Environment (UNCHE) in Stockholm which concluded by identifying the need for states to work cooperatively to solve environmental issues on a global scale (United Nations, 2025a). The World Meteorological Organization (WMO) organised the First World Climate
Conference in 1979 and this called for the urgent development of “a common strategy for a greater understanding if the climate system and a rational use of climate information” and there was also the proposal to establish the World Climate Programme (WCP) that apply understanding of the climate system “for the benefit of societies coping with climate variability and change” (World Meteorological Organization, 2025 - online).
The WMO and the United Nations Environment Programme established the Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change (IPCC) in 1988 (IPCC, 2025a) with an initial task that was outlined in UN General Assembly Resolution 43/53 of 6 December 1988 ( IPCC, 2025b – page 134) as follows:
“to prepare a comprehensive review and recommendations with respect to: (a) the state of knowledge of the science of climate change; (b) ......the social and economic impact of climate change including global warming, (c ) possible response strategies to delay, limit or mitigate the impact of adverse climate change; (d) the identification and possible strengthening of relevant existing international legal instruments having a bearing on climate (and)
(e ) elements for inclusion in a possible future international convention on climate.”
The decade of the 1990s witnessed major developments in global attitudes to climate change concerns. The United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change (UNFCCC) is a multilateral treaty adopted in 1992 and entered into force in 1994 with the purpose of stabilizing greenhouse gas concentrations “at a level that would prevent dangerous anthropogenic (human-induced) interference with the climate system” (United Nations, 2025a, online). The UNFCCC is a framework that has provided the basis for international climate negotiations including the landmark agreements such as the Kyoto Protocol (1997) and the Paris Agreement (2015) that have been drivers of climate change policy in many of the world’s countries including the UK.
There are currently 198 Parties to the UNFCCC (United Nations, 2025b), these are known as ‘Parties to the Convention’, and there is an annual Conference of the Parties (COP) with the stated objectives of measuring progress and negotiating multilateral responses to climate change. The first COP was held in Berlin, Germany in 1995 and at the time of writing the most recent COP was COP29 that was held in Baku, Azerbaijan, in November 2024 – a historical summary of the COPs is shown in Figure A in the Appendix. There have been major challenges over the thirty years of COPs in achieving consensus on more than broad principles and in some years, such as COP 6 in 2000 and COP 18 in 2012 there have been


