IV The Paris Agreement (2015)
The Paris Agreement (United Nations, 2015) was signed by Parties at COP 21 in Paris on 12 December 2015, coming into force on 4 November 2016 as a legally binding international treaty on climate change (UNFCCC, 2025b). The date of 4 November 2016 is a precise date determined as “...thirty days after the date on which 55 Parties to the Convention accounting in total for at least an estimated 55% of the total global greenhouse gas emissions have deposited their instruments of ratification, acceptance approval or accession with the Depositary” (UNFCCC, 2025c). At the time of writing, 195 Parties out of 198
Parties to the UNFCCC are Parties to the Paris Agreement. There are a number of key terms of the Paris Agreement and obligations on the signatory nations:
1. Global temperature increase is to be limited to well below 2 Degrees Celsius and at the same time efforts are to be pursued to limit global temperature increase to 1.5 Degrees Celsius above pre-industrial levels. 2. Each nation is required to determine and submit its own climate action plan in the form of Nationally Determined Contributions (NDCs) set its own emission reduction targets that are reviewed every five years to raise ambitions. 3. Richer nations are expected to help poorer nations with climate finance to adapt to climate change and transition to renewable energy. Climate finance is presented as a crucial part of building resilience and reducing the vulnerability of poorer nations.
There are also other significant features of the Paris Agreement, for instance: 1. Technology transfer, development and cooperation is to be encouraged. 2. Transparency is a fundamental feature to allow progress and accountability to be tracked.
The US is the most significant nation in a very short list of nations that are not Parties to the Paris Agreement (at the time of writing). The US officially joined the Paris Agreement in September 2016 but then officially withdrew from the Agreement in 2020 during the administration of President Trump. The US then re-joined the Agreement in 2021 during the administration of President Biden. More recently, following another change of administration, the US White House announced on 20 January 2025 that it would withdraw from the Paris Agreement (The White House, 2025).
