VI Carbon Pricing Systems around the World
There are broadly three main forms of carbon pricing: carbon taxes, emissions trading systems and carbon crediting mechanisms. The theoretical bases that often inform government policies will be discussed in brief terms before moving on to a discussion about where they are being applied in global carbon markets (for a more detailed discussion see Kirkpatrick, 2022).
Carbon Taxes – A Brief Outline and Basic Theory
Carbon Taxes are indirect taxes as they are usually paid to governments by producers and are likely to be borne by the end consumer who is therefore indirectly paying the carbon tax. Carbon taxes are also likely to have an inflationary effect, at least a temporary inflationary effect that may become permanent depending for example on how a government uses the tax revenues. Carbon taxes may be considered as the creation of a price signal in response to negative externalities (Pigou, 1920 and later contributors) although the problems of tax distortions and deadweight loss have been introduced and analysed by for example, Hicks (1941) and Harberger (1964 and 1971) while the relevance and efficacy of carbon taxes has also been challenged (Coase, 1960). Carbon taxes may also be unpopular if the taxpayers perceive that such forms of carbon pricing threaten national competitiveness and the economic growth objective. The problem of ‘free-riding’ by nations that do not impose their own carbon taxes has been discussed by for example, MacKay et al. (2017) and Nordhaus (2015 and 2017) who suggested that such concerns were a driver of the creation of ‘climate clubs’. Carbon Border Adjustment Mechanisms (CBAMs), discussed in section VIII below) are a response to concerns about ‘free-riding’.
Emissions Trading Systems or ETSs – A Brief Outline and Basic Theory
ETSs are otherwise known as ‘cap-and-trade’ systems because they work by a regulatory body such as a government department setting a cap on carbon emissions or other GHG emissions and distributing allowances to businesses that may then trade those allowances that are often in the form of certificates. ETSs and carbon taxes both serve to ‘put a price’ on CO2 emissions but there are key differences in that while carbon taxes may prioritise certainty of price, ETSs prioritise certainty of quantity so the market determines the price based on forces of demand and supply. It is important to point out that in practice these systems may not conform exactly to the theoretical expectations and there are also variants of the cap-and-trade instrument where there may be a hybrid approach so that an ETS may have features of price control such as a guaranteed ceiling and/or floor price (Kirkpatrick, 2022).
Carbon Crediting Systems – A Brief Outline and Basic Theory
Carbon Crediting Systems share more features with ETSs than with carbon taxes. Like ETSs Carbon Crediting Systems are designed to have an impact on the quantity of CO2 emissions rather than impacting price directly like carbon taxes. Carbon credits are created by projects for various GHGs including reducing or removing CO2 from the atmosphere, including sustainable agriculture, peatland restoration, reforestation and afforestation, and carbon capture and storage. Carbon crediting is discussed more in Section VII below.
