Carbon Markets The development of carbon pricing mechanisms in the form of emissions trading systems has allowed carbon markets to develop. There are broadly two forms of carbon markets: the first, ‘mandatory carbon markets’ or ‘compliance carbon markets (CCMs) that include
the China National ETS, the EU ETS and the UK ETS that have been created and subsequently are regulated by nations or states (as in the USA) or in the case of the EU, a union of
national authorities; and the second, ‘voluntary carbon markets’ (VCMs ) are not regulated.
Mandatory or Compliance Carbon Markets (CCMs) For CCMs the commodity is in the form of carbon allowances or carbon credits. Carbon allowances are permits to emit CO2 and as facilitated by a cap-and -trade system. Carbon credits are also created by governments, at least that is usually the case, and they are a measurement unit by which a business or organisation may be repaid for investing in
carbon-reducing initiatives or ‘green projects’. CCMs are regulated in accordance with a specific ETS to comply with the country’s NDCs under the Paris Agreement (2015) with mandatory participation by ‘in scope’ entities.
Carbon prices in CCMs are determined by processes of the specific ETS operating according to its emissions cap, and the market forces of supply and demand for emissions allowances in international carbon markets. The participants are the businesses that are ‘in scope’ and issues with carbon allowances or credits within the ETS. The carbon allowances usually take the form of certificates each granting permission to generate one ton of CO2 emissions. The marketplace is a regulated market set by cap-and-trade regulations at regional or state level. The exchanges such as the Intercontinental Exchange (ICE) and the European Energy Exchange (EEX) offer platforms for continuous trading so carbon allowances can be traded throughout the day.
The mandatory carbon markets in some of the major CO2 emitting jurisdictions are discussed briefly below – please note that at the time of writing many countries are considering the introduction of CP instrument and it is not practical to consider all nascent markets here. It is worth pointing out that China, USA and the EU nations together account for significantly more than half of the world’s total anthropogenic CO2 emissions (World Bank, 2025b).
China: China National Emissions Trading Scheme (or China National Carbon Market) started operating in 2021. It was reported in 2024 to be the largest emissions trading system operating in the world in terms of covered emissions that are estimated to be around 8 billion tCO2 (ICAP, 2024b). The China National ETS covers 51 per cent. of the jurisdiction’s emissions based on data available by 2025 (World Bank, 2025b). There are also Pilot ETSs operating in a number of the Chinese provinces that are as follows (with coverage of jurisdiction CO2 emissions in brackets: Beijing (17%); Chongqing (14%); Fujian (16%); Guangdong (12%); Hubei (20%); Shanghai (21%); Shenzhen (37%); and Tianjin (18%).


