USA: In the USA there are a number of ETSs operating in different states but there is to date no overarching nationwide ETS. The California Cap-and-Trade or ‘CaT’ programme is a market-based system with a declining limit on CO2 emissions set by the California Air Resources Board (CARB). The California Global Warming Solutions Act (USA) started operating in 2012 with a jurisdiction that covers only the State of California. Sectors regulated in the First Phase were large industrial facilities such as cement, glass, hydrogen, iron and steel; electricity generation; electricity imports; other stationary combustion; CO2 suppliers; and then in a Second Phase further sectors were added: suppliers of natural gas; suppliers of certain distillate fuel oils; suppliers of liquid petroleum gas; and, suppliers of liquefied natural gas.
The Washington Climate Commitment Act signed into law in 2021 led to Washington State’s ETS or ‘cap-and-invest program’ starting to operate in January 2023 covering 71% of the state’s emissions (see Table 7) with a cap trajectory to reduce the state’s GHG emissions to a level 95% below 1990 levels by 2050. Sectors covered are mining and extractives, transport, buildings, industry and power ICAP, 2024b). The Oregon ETS started operating in 2025 with a cap for GHGs of 24.1 MtCO2e in 2025 and covering the sectors: agriculture and forestry, maritime, mining and extractives, waste, transport, buildings and industry (ICAP, 2024b). The Regional Greenhouse Gas Initiative (RGGI) was formed in 2009 and was the first mandatory carbon market GHG ETS in the USA and initially covered ten states and though there have been some changes over the years – for example Virginia joined in 2021 but left in 2023 - the founding states are still the membership in 2025: Connecticut, Delaware, Maine, Maryland, Massachusetts, New Hampshire, New Jersey, New York, Rhode Island, and Vermont. The RGGI had a cap of 63 MtCO2 in 2024 covering the power sector (ICAP, 2024b). The Colorado ETS was formed when the Colorado Air Quality Control Commission or AQCC adopted regulations to establish an ETS for large manufacturers in October 2023 and this scope is to be expanded following a decision in December 2024 to include oil and gas operations from 2028. A distinguishing feature of the Colorado ETS is that it uses a hybrid approach combining both absolute emissions and emissions-intensity targets. The ETS is based on regulations for Greenhouse Gas Emissions and Energy Management for Manufacturing (GEMM) that were developed after 2021 so that GEMM 1 a category covering energy-intensive and trade-exposed manufacturers emitting 25,000 tCO2e or more annually and is regulated based on emissions intensity with targets for improving GHG per unit of output. GEMM 2 is a second category covering all other manufacturing facilities emitting 25,000 tCO2e or more annually, regulated by reference to absolute emissions and subject to an emissions cap.
EU/Europe: The European Market for Carbon Credits was the world’s first and by 2025 among the largest carbon markets in the world (European Commission, 2025). The EU ETS provides carbon credit contracts for trading and delivering European Union Allowances (EUAs) where one EUA allows the holder to emit one ton of CO2 or CO2 equivalent in greenhouse gas. The EUA Market has both a primary market and a secondary market. The primary market includes the free allocation of a portion of the total EUAs to operators, a specific fund reserve for certain projects and all remaining EUAs are sold through auctions that are run by auction platforms for continuous trading mostly on the Intercontinental Exchange (ICE) and the European Energy Exchange (EEX). EUAs are classed as derivatives


