Voluntary Carbon Markets VCMs) Certain types of products are relevant for investment in the VCMs. It has been argued that nature-based carbon markets can provide a significant contribution to ‘carbon mitigation’ required by 2030 to keep the 1.5 degree target within reach (Moore et al, 2023). Although there is no formal or direct regulation of VCMs there have been some discussions about introducing measures to ensure there is transparency and integrity in the VCMs are they do not become vehicles for greenwashing.
In VCMs the commodity is the form of ‘carbon offsets’ resulting from carbon sequestration to remove CO2 from the Earth’s atmosphere and hold it in solid or liquid form. The VCM is self-regulated so there is no formal or direct regulation by governments or financial authorities. The VCM is a nascent market and there are issues to consider, for example, there are discussions in most jurisdictions about how matters such as transparency and integrity need to be addressed. Carbon prices in VCMs are determined in a specific project with chosen participants. The prices are driven by supply and demand as well as specific project characteristics, buyer preferences and the quality of each project, so for example, those projects certified under rigorous standards for biodiversity, community contribution, and climate impact are more likely to command higher prices.
The participants in VCMs are businesses that take the opportunity to work with similarly motivated businesses or individuals to offset their carbon emissions. Throughout the 2020s here has been increasing interest in the VCM by participants including oil and gas majors, hedge funds and banks especially those that have financial structuring and commodity trading expertise. The VCMs may be distinguished from the CCMs that have organised regulated markets, as VCMs do not have a centralized market. In a VCM project developers or businesses sell carbon offsets to buyers through brokers, retailers or using digital platforms.
A significant development in VCMs has come in the form of initiatives by Shell and Boston Consulting Group that show the strategic importance of major purchasers of carbon offsets in the VCM. It has been estimated that the VCM is growing and is set to grow more although precise estimates are hard to pin down, for example the Boston Consulting Group estimated that the value of the VCM could reach between USD10 billion and USD40 billion by 2030 (Porsberg-Smith et al, 2023). Carbon pricing can seem like a largely national decision but there is scope for most nations to affect and be affected by economic activities in other countries – it is a highly interconnected world.


