Carbon Allowances, Carbon Credits and Carbon Offsets
Some care is needed when discussing ‘carbon allowances’, ‘carbon credits’ and ‘carbon offsets’ – the term ‘carbon credits’ may be found to be used interchangeably with either ‘carbon allowances’ or ‘carbon offsets’ depending on the sources and any particular author’s interpretation. The terms ‘carbon crediting’ and carbon credits are often used to
describe the arrangements that are created by governments (or their delegated bodies) and are transacted in CCMs – but confusingly the terms such as ‘offset system’ or ‘credit offset’ are also used for certain government schemes in certain nations. Carbon offsets are also created by independent businesses (not governments) and are transacted in VCMs.
VIII The Development of CBAMs
According to World Bank data only 28 per cent of carbon emissions were covered by carbon pricing mechanisms in 2025 (World Bank, 2025a) . There are no doubt different views on whether this is closer to success or failure after the major international efforts over many years including about 30 ‘COPs’. With around 72 per cent of carbon emissions not covered by any form of carbon pricing system as implied from the World Bank (2025a) figures there may be a significant risk of ‘carbon leakage’ and other adverse implications for nations
operating carbon pricing in a world that has not achieved global carbon pricing despite the laudable intentions.
Countries may decide to operate carbon pricing policies to deter ‘free-riding’ by jurisdictions that do not apply carbon pricing (MacKay et al., 2017). The idea of ‘Climate Clubs’ and the rationale for the imposition of carbon tariffs has been discussed by for example, Nordhaus (2017). The drive towards a global carbon pricing objective is a continuing process. At the time of writing only one carbon border adjustment mechanism (CBAM) is operating – the EU CBAM – and there has been some research into the reactions to this by the nations outside the EU, for example, the IETA found in a 2024 study that potential support for CBAMs varies across countries and income levels (IETA, 2024).
Other CBAMs are expected to be implemented in the next few years, for example, the UK is scheduled to introduce a CBAM in January 2027 (HM Treasury, 2025). The UK CBAM is likely to directly affect businesses that import GBP50,000 or more of specified goods from the aluminium, cement, fertiliser, hydrogen, and, iron and steel sectors over a 12-month period, while businesses that use such goods in their supply chain will be indirectly impacted, so this is expected to impact an estimated 10,000 businesses and will also impose on SMEs proportionately higher administrative costs relative to larger producers (HMRC, 2025). Another jurisdiction that may introduce a CBAM is Canada where ‘Border Carbon Adjustments’ (BCAs) are being explored by the Government of Canada to provide a tool to complement existing domestic carbon pricing instruments (Natural Resources Canada, 2025). It may not be surprising if Canada is considering entering bilateral agreements with the EU (IETA, 2024).
There are a number of significant jurisdictions in terms of the sizes of their economies and their GHGs including CO2 emissions that are in the process of developing their own carbon pricing mechanisms and it may be that CBAMs will also be introduced in due course.


