IX Some Controversies and Challenges in Carbon Pricing and Carbon Markets
The history of carbon pricing developments over about 50 years has shown that there are considerable controversies and challenges in achieving global carbon pricing that is consistent, far less uniform. Some of these are mentioned below.
1. The Kyoto Protocol (1997) discussed earlier, introduced the concept of national carbon emissions trading and the Clean Development Mechanism (CDM) but even though this appeared to be an achievement in reaching international cooperation the treaty has faced criticism as a case of institutional failure with inadequate design features such as too short a time frame for action, static targets for emission reductions, the form of calculation of emission reductions (CDMs), and multiple commitment periods perpetuating path-dependent structures (Rosen, 2015). It could be argued that a strength of the Kyoto Protocol was in helping to ensure that climate change remained on the international agenda but some of the major challenges and problems that confronted Kyoto have remained for The Paris Agreement (2015) such as problems of relevance and enforcement when significant carbon emitting countries are not members of the Paris Agreement as at 2025.
2. There are differing views on CBAMs – CBAMs are technically very important for the effectiveness of carbon pricing but there is considerable resistance due to international trade relations effects arising from concerns about economic impact on both exporting and importing countries.
3. Price transparency is an important feature to promote growth in carbon markets and there is a challenge especially in the VCM where the wide array of unique projects presents some complexity or even barriers for traders seeking accurate daily pricing for profit and loss and mark-to-market valuation purposes.
4. The VCM by its nature is subject to variations in carbon offset project quality and concerns over quality assurance so that potential buyers may be deterred if the carbon offsets are shown to have been oversold by subsequent research evidence.
5. Carbon pricing that includes carbon taxes and emissions trading systems can have
regressive effects on possibly sizeable sections of a nation’s population especially the lower income but also the middle income or ‘middle wealthy’ within the population and the theories of compensatory recycling of tax revenues or revenues from sales of carbon allowances may not always occur in practice.
6. After many years of international institutional discussions only 28 per cent of the world’s carbon emissions by 2025 (World Bank, 2025a) were subject to carbon pricing systems.
7. Measuring carbon emissions and the effectiveness of carbon pricing systems is complex and prone to error and manipulation – there remain many questions as to how these problems can be addressed in the future.


